# denylist.example — copy to denylist.local and replace with your own terms.
#
#   cp denylist.example denylist.local
#
# denylist.local is gitignored. The terms you are trying to keep out of a
# public repository are themselves the sensitive part, so they live in an
# untracked file and check.sh reads them from there.
#
# Sourced as shell, so each variable is a single-quoted extended-regex blob.
# Each line is trimmed and the lines are joined with |, so put one term per
# line and lay it out however reads best. Interior spaces survive, so
# multi-word terms work — but do not pad the | separators with spaces.
# An empty string disables that scan. An invalid pattern is a hard error, not
# a silent pass.

# Case-insensitive. Repository names, service codenames, internal tools,
# people and agent names, and any domain vocabulary specific enough to
# identify the origin of the text.
INSENSITIVE='
internal-repo-name|service-codename|legacy-system-name
\bteammate-name\b|\borg-name\b
domain-term-one|domain-term-two|domain-term-three
path/to/internal/module|internal_entry_point\.py
'

# Case-sensitive. Terms whose lowercase form is ordinary English and would
# otherwise fire on innocent prose.
SENSITIVE='Tier A|ACRONYM'

# Measured figures from the private work. A published number turns an
# unfalsifiable claim into a fingerprint.
NUMBERS='\b12,?345\b|\b67 findings\b'

# Letters outside ASCII that would reveal your working language. List only
# the letters specific to it — a blanket non-ASCII test fires on em dashes,
# arrows and typographic quotes, which are fine in English text.
# Empty by default so the check is opt-in.
LOCALE_CHARS=''

# Everything above is matched against file contents, commit messages and ref
# names. This last one is different in kind.
#
# Every commit's author and committer must appear here, formatted exactly as
# git records it: `Name <email>`, one per line. It is an allowlist, not a
# denylist — anything not listed fails. That inversion is deliberate. A
# denylist can only catch an identity someone thought to write down, and the
# leak this exists to prevent was a second machine's username that happened to
# be on the list by luck. Next time it will not be.
#
# Empty disables the check, and check.sh will say so rather than implying the
# authorship was verified.
EXPECTED_IDENTITY='Your Name <you@example.com>'
